From: Daniel Pawlik <pawlik.dan@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: npu: use coherent DMA for mailbox buffers

Commit 6f884eb87a79 ("net: airoha: Fix DMA direction for NPU mailbox
buffer") switched airoha_npu_send_msg() from DMA_TO_DEVICE to
DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL so non-coherent EN7581 CPUs invalidate cache before
reading NPU GET responses.

On EN7581 + MT7996 (eagle) that change regresses probe: mailbox DONE
succeeds but WLAN_FUNC_GET_WAIT_NPU_VERSION still reads as 0.0, so
mt76 never binds NPU offload. Likely DONE vs DRAM visibility after the
extra invalidate, or mixed coherency on the mailbox path.

Avoid streaming-DMA direction issues entirely for these small messages:
copy through a dma_alloc_coherent() buffer for the mailbox transaction,
then copy the response back to the caller's buffer. That matches how
many drivers handle tiny command/response mailboxes and restores a
stable 0.1111 version read with or without 6f884eb.

Fixes: 6f884eb87a79 ("net: airoha: Fix DMA direction for NPU mailbox buffer")
Signed-off-by: Daniel Pawlik <pawlik.dan@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_npu.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_npu.c
@@ -166,12 +166,16 @@ static int airoha_npu_send_msg(struct ai
 	u16 core = 0; /* FIXME */
 	u32 val, offset = core << 4;
 	dma_addr_t dma_addr;
+	void *dma_buf;
 	int ret;
 
-	dma_addr = dma_map_single(npu->dev, p, size, DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL);
-	ret = dma_mapping_error(npu->dev, dma_addr);
-	if (ret)
-		return ret;
+	/* Small cmd/rsp mailbox: coherent buffer avoids TO_DEVICE vs
+	 * BIDIRECTIONAL cache pitfalls on EN7581 (see 6f884eb87a79).
+	 */
+	dma_buf = dma_alloc_coherent(npu->dev, size, &dma_addr, GFP_ATOMIC);
+	if (!dma_buf)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+	memcpy(dma_buf, p, size);
 
 	spin_lock_bh(&npu->cores[core].lock);
 
@@ -191,7 +195,9 @@ static int airoha_npu_send_msg(struct ai
 
 	spin_unlock_bh(&npu->cores[core].lock);
 
-	dma_unmap_single(npu->dev, dma_addr, size, DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL);
+	if (!ret)
+		memcpy(p, dma_buf, size);
+	dma_free_coherent(npu->dev, size, dma_buf, dma_addr);
 
 	return ret;
 }
